 King  Solomon is definitely the person you  need to talk to he can take care of that  situation for you but the need for  personal healing is not a problem for  Kings to solve even in an age of  government health care people who are  ready need to get care at a hospital not  a capital if you need mental healing you  need to go to a mental health  professional and if you need spiritual  healing you need to go somewhere where  you can get spiritual help naman needed  the kind of healing that only a prophet  could provide he went to Israel in the  first place because an Israeli girl who  worked at his house told him about the  amazing profit there who could heal him  but the first way that naman resisted  his own healing was to go to see a king  instead like many of us naman avoided  spiritual solutions because he was more  comfortable dealing with things that  were familiar power dynamics he was used  to navigating transactions that fit  easily into a sense of how the world  worked he liked to feel in control of  the situation he had worked closely with  the King back in Aaron so when he went  to visit the king of Israel he could  stay in his professional persona he  could talk about healing as if it were a  political problem or a business  transaction the only problem was that  the King had no idea how to heal him  when we need spiritual healing we need  to make sure we're looking in the right  places lesson number two healing isn't  always a spectacular and instantaneous  event don't expect an earth-shattering  moment in which he show up and someone  does something to  and suddenly everything's okay even in  the Bible healing is not magic after the  King can't help him neiman does go to  the Prophet Elisha but when Elijah sends  out a servant to tell him to watch seven  times in the river naman becomes angry  and goes away saying I thought that for  me he would surely come out and stand  and call on the name of the Lord his God  and a wave his hand over the spot and  cured the leprosy naman is frustrated  for many reasons he wants a dramatic  encounter with a powerful person but  instead he gets a message from a servant  he wants a magical moment in which oh I  show waves his arm like Harry Potter  waving a wand but instead he is told to  go take a bath he wants ceiling to  happen right away but instead he is told  that there's more that has to happen  first Elijah's offer of healing doesn't  fit neimans pre-existing idea of what  healing is supposed to look like it's  not glamorous it's not exciting it's a  little humbling so named and turns it  down lessons number three and four don't  expect the healing process to be  convenient and don't wait to do it until  it makes sense after turning down  hellacious prescription of bathing in  the Jordan naman starts complaining are  not abana and fire fire the rivers of  Damascus better than all the waters of  Israel he asks can I not wash them them  and be clean Bailey in the Jordan River  is inconvenient and it doesn't make  sense logically there's no reason to  bathe in the Jordan you can take about  anywhere name is probably thinking if I  can't get healing directly from the King  and if I can't have the dramatic magical  America like rave at least I want to get  healed close to home in a river I know  and like I think neimans response gives  us some insight into some of the ways we  might resist healing and some of the  excuses we might use to keep it at bay I  know I can relate to name in here I can  easily imagine saying  I want my healing process to make total  sense to me and ideally I'd like it to  happen in my neighborhood and if I can't  have it on those terms I don't want it  at all unfortunately as name and  discovers we can't always have hailing  on our own terms and at this point in  the story it looks like he's settling  for no healing at all what's the number  five listen to your community it's at  exactly this point the name and servants  decide to stage an intervention and the  story ceases to head towards tragedy and  takes a turn towards the miraculous name  and servants a father if the Prophet had  commanded you to do something difficult  but you not have done it how much more  when all you said to you was wash and be  clean this summer I taught a writing  class at the med school my students were  pre-med students working on personal  statements for the medical applications  and their first assignment was to write  about a time when they were a patient it  turns out that pre-med students are  often terrible patients they are grated  doing hard things like organic chemistry  but when they have the flu and their  doctor tells them to do something easy  like rust and sleep and drink fluids  they say no way I am going to power it  through the flu one of my students were  at a time and she was trying to power  through the classes within 103 degree  fever and a roommates had to sit her  down and say look you're sick it's time  for you to go to bed and drink some tea  sometimes that are insisting on doing  things the hard way and trying to heal  ourselves through sheer willpower and  stand that we need to stop and listen to  our community the people around us who  care about our well-being sometimes when  we're resisting healing they can help us  see what we need to do which brings us  to lesson number six embrace the healing  power of ritual thankfully name and  story doesn't end with his roof  ulta babe eventually naman listens to  the people around him and decides to  give healing a chance he goes to the  Jordan River and he walks in and he  washes himself and then he does it again  and again and again and again and again  and again and then he looks down at  himself and he sees that his flesh is  restored and he is clean ritual is a  powerful thing you don't have to  understand it for it to work all you  have to do is choose to participate when  our desire to maintain control can hold  us back when we're demanding  instantaneous solutions participating in  ritual can help to make us whole we can  believe in ritual even when we can't  believe in anything else through  participation and repetition we can  bypass our insistence on intellectual  understanding and allow God's grace to  heal us very healed through the water of  baptism the bread and wine of communion  the weekly rituals of our worship  together as a community and our  individual worship with just us and God  but we're also healed through quotidian  rituals we perform every day if we're  suffering from perfectionism then every  time we show up to take a midterm or  open up our laptop to write a paper we  can imagine that we are walking to the  bank of the River Jordan and gently  immersing ourselves and it's healing  streams if we are crippled by our fear  of future then every time we send out a  job application or show up at an  interview we can think about how the  waters of the Jordan are washing our  fears away name and washed in the Jordan  7 times in the Bible 7 is a special  number sometimes it means 7 sometimes it  means infinity majitha said we should  forgive people seventy times seven he  didn't mean that we should forgive them  for  90 times he meant we should just keep on  forgiving healing rituals are part of a  lifelong process that needs to keep on  happening like meals and showers these  rituals of healing need to be part of  our daily lives our seventh and last  lesson of the day is that praise is part  of the process name and story doesn't  end when he walks out of the river with  healthy skin it doesn't end with him  heading home and living exactly like he  did before instead it ends with him  saying from now on I'm gonna praise God  neimans guardedness has been transformed  to gratitude he even asked for some dirt  to take home from the place where he was  healed so he can always remember what  happened to him in Israel he is never  going to forget what God did for him  sisters and brothers the good news of  name and story is that healing is real  it was real for him and it is real for  us the challenge of neimans story is  that sometimes our defense mechanisms  can prevent us from receiving healing  when it is offered to us but name and  story teaches us that we don't need to  feel ready we don't need to wait or get  angry or turn away we don't need money  or power we don't need to be worthy we  just need to listen to our friends and  participate in the healing process in  the words of the hymn let not conscience  make you linger nor fitness fondly dream  all the fitness Christ required is to  feel your need of him let's venture on  God like naman let's finally set aside  our fears and hesitations our need for  control and let's learn to embrace the  healing that's ours and then let's spend  our lives and gratitude and praise Amen
 

